Task 1: Create and Configure ApplicationSets
Subtask 1.1: Understanding ApplicationSets
ApplicationSets are a powerful ArgoCD feature that allows you to deploy applications across multiple clusters automatically. Think of it as a template that creates multiple ArgoCD Applications based on defined patterns.

Key Concepts:

ApplicationSet Controller: Manages the lifecycle of ApplicationSets
Generators: Define how to create multiple applications from templates
Template: The blueprint for creating ArgoCD Applications
Subtask 1.2: Set Up the Lab Environment
First, let's verify our environment and set up the necessary components.

# Check if kubectl is working
kubectl version --client

# Verify cluster access
kubectl cluster-info

# Check available contexts (clusters)
kubectl config get-contexts
Subtask 1.3: Install ArgoCD
Let's install ArgoCD in our primary cluster:

# Create ArgoCD namespace
kubectl create namespace argocd

# Install ArgoCD
kubectl apply -n argocd -f https://raw.githubusercontent.com/argoproj/argo-cd/stable/manifests/install.yaml

# Wait for ArgoCD to be ready
kubectl wait --for=condition=available --timeout=300s deployment/argocd-server -n argocd

# Verify ArgoCD installation
kubectl get pods -n argocd
Subtask 1.4: Access ArgoCD UI
# Get ArgoCD admin password
kubectl -n argocd get secret argocd-initial-admin-secret -o jsonpath="{.data.password}" | base64 -d && echo

# Port forward to access ArgoCD UI
kubectl port-forward svc/argocd-server -n argocd 8080:443 &

# Note: ArgoCD UI will be available at https://localhost:8080
# Username: admin
# Password: (from the command above)
Subtask 1.5: Install ApplicationSet Controller
# Install ApplicationSet controller
kubectl apply -n argocd -f https://raw.githubusercontent.com/argoproj/applicationset/v0.4.1/manifests/install.yaml

# Verify ApplicationSet controller is running
kubectl get pods -n argocd | grep applicationset
Subtask 1.6: Create Sample Application Repository
Let's create a Git repository structure for our applications:

# Create a directory for our Git repository
mkdir -p ~/multi-cluster-apps
cd ~/multi-cluster-apps

# Initialize Git repository
git init

# Create application manifests directory
mkdir -p apps/guestbook
mkdir -p clusters

# Create a simple guestbook application manifest
cat > apps/guestbook/deployment.yaml << 'EOF'
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: guestbook
  labels:
    app: guestbook
spec:
  replicas: 2
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: guestbook
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: guestbook
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: guestbook
        image: gcr.io/heptio-images/ks-guestbook-demo:0.2
        ports:
        - containerPort: 3000
        env:
        - name: PORT
          value: "3000"
---
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: guestbook-service
spec:
  selector:
    app: guestbook
  ports:
  - port: 80
    targetPort: 3000
  type: ClusterIP
EOF
